{
  "concept": "Electrophilic bromination of benzene passes through two activation barriers and a cationic arenium intermediate, and the substitution branch is observed (while addition is not) because substitution ends downhill in free energy whereas addition ends uphill.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "pale blue rectangular panel enclosing the diagram",
    "vertical gradient arrow at far left (pale yellow at bottom to orange at top) labeled 'Energy'",
    "horizontal axis label 'Reaction progress' with a magenta rightward arrow",
    "dashed horizontal reactant energy level at left",
    "benzene hexagon (reactant structure)",
    "plus sign between benzene and Br2",
    "magenta 'Br2' label",
    "black substitution energy curve (reactants -> TS1 -> trough -> TS2 -> products)",
    "first (higher) transition-state peak with a short dashed cap line",
    "vertical double-headed arrow labeled 'ΔG‡' between first peak and reactant level",
    "trough holding the cationic arenium intermediate",
    "arenium intermediate structure: partly unsaturated six-membered ring with H and magenta Br on the same carbon and a '+' sign above",
    "second (lower) transition-state peak on the black curve",
    "dashed horizontal product energy level below the reactant level",
    "bromobenzene product structure (benzene ring with magenta Br)",
    "'HBr' label and plus sign next to bromobenzene",
    "'Substitution' label under the product level",
    "small vertical double-headed arrow labeled 'ΔG°' spanning the reactant-to-product level gap",
    "magenta addition energy curve branching upward from the same trough over its own maximum",
    "bracketed dibrominated ring structure at the addition endpoint (above reactant level)",
    "'Addition' label and magenta '(does not occur)' label under the bracketed ring"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "reactionStage",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step along substitution path",
      "states": ["reactants", "transitionState1", "intermediate", "transitionState2", "products"],
      "default": "reactants",
      "teaches": "Stepping through the black curve shows the reaction climbing a first barrier to a cationic arenium intermediate, then a second smaller barrier, before falling to substitution products — i.e. two distinct activation barriers bracket one shared intermediate"
    },
    {
      "id": "pathway",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Pathway",
      "options": ["Substitution", "Addition"],
      "default": "Substitution",
      "teaches": "Both branches share the same first barrier and arenium intermediate, but the substitution branch finishes below the reactant level (downhill, occurs) while the addition branch finishes above it (uphill, does not occur) — the relative endpoint energy decides which pathway is observed"
    },
    {
      "id": "energyArrow",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking the tall arrow emphasizes ΔG‡ (reactant level to first peak = activation energy, a kinetic barrier), while clicking the short arrow emphasizes ΔG° (reactant level to product level = overall reaction free energy, a thermodynamic driving force); the two measure different vertical gaps on the same energy scale"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Reactants on the energy scale",
      "narration": "Benzene and Br2 sit on the dashed reactant level at the left of a single vertical energy scale; everything that follows is read as a height on this scale.",
      "state": { "pathway": "Substitution", "reactionStage": "reactants" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two barriers, one intermediate",
      "narration": "Step the black curve forward: it climbs the tall first barrier (ΔG‡) into the cationic arenium intermediate in the trough, then a second smaller barrier, before dropping to products — substitution is a two-step, two-barrier process.",
      "state": { "pathway": "Substitution", "reactionStage": "intermediate" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Substitution ends downhill",
      "narration": "At the products stage the bromobenzene + HBr level lies below the reactants, so ΔG° is negative and the substitution branch is thermodynamically favored.",
      "state": { "pathway": "Substitution", "reactionStage": "products" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why addition does not occur",
      "narration": "Toggle to the magenta addition branch: it leaves the same intermediate but climbs to a dibrominated product that sits above the reactant level — an uphill endpoint, which is why this pathway does not occur.",
      "state": { "pathway": "Addition", "reactionStage": "intermediate" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Activation energy vs overall free energy",
      "narration": "Click the tall arrow to highlight ΔG‡ (kinetic barrier to the first peak) and the short arrow to highlight ΔG° (thermodynamic gap to the products); they are different vertical distances on the same scale and answer different questions.",
      "state": { "pathway": "Substitution", "reactionStage": "products" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Substitution and addition share the same first barrier and arenium intermediate, so the deciding factor is the final step's thermodynamics: substitution ends below the reactant level (downhill, occurs) while addition ends above it (uphill, does not occur).",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Lay out a pale blue panel with a left-edge vertical gradient arrow (pale yellow bottom to orange top) labeled 'Energy' and a bottom 'Reaction progress' label ending in a magenta arrow. Draw the black substitution curve as a smooth two-hump profile: tall first peak (dashed cap), deep trough, lower second peak, then a steep fall to a dashed product level clearly below the dashed reactant level. Place the benzene + magenta 'Br2' at the reactant level, the arenium cation (ring with H and magenta Br on one carbon, '+' above) in the trough, and bromobenzene + 'HBr' over the word 'Substitution' at the product level. From the same trough draw a magenta curve rising over its own maximum to a bracketed dibrominated ring above the reactant level, labeled 'Addition' with magenta '(does not occur)'. Add the vertical double-headed 'ΔG‡' arrow between the first peak and reactant level and the small 'ΔG°' double-headed arrow across the reactant-product gap. Use black for the substitution curve and magenta for the addition curve and all Br-related text so the two branches and the bromine atoms read consistently; keep both endpoint levels on one shared vertical scale since that comparison is the entire argument."
}

